** Dynamic h-adaptivity regression test -- exercises the full *modelModifier path end to end:
** marking -> octree refinement -> model mutation -> nodal warm-start -> quadrature-point state
** transfer -> hanging-node MPC -> equation-system rebuild -> solve on the refined mesh.
** Two coarse GC3D20R elements form a cantilever; bending concentrates stress at the fixed end,
** so the stress marker refines exactly one element (2 -> 9 active, 13 hanging nodes), creating a
** 2:1 hanging interface that the MPC couples across both fields (displacement + nonlocal damage).
** Pins the dynamic solution against U.ref. (A true adaptive-vs-uniform-fine PHYSICS benchmark --
** PLAN_AMR.md M3 -- needs a two-run comparison harness and remains future work.)
*material, name=AT2PHASEFIELD, id=mat
20000, 0.2, 0.1, 5

*section, name=sec, material=mat, type=solid
all

*modelModifier, type=hAdaptivity, name=amr
>>marker, type=fieldOutput, fieldOutput=stressForAMR, expression='abs(x) > 0.6'
maxLevel=1

*job, name=amr, domain=3d
*solver, solver=NIST, name=S
*fieldOutput
>>perNode, elSet=all, field=displacement, result=U, name=U
>>perNode, elSet=all, field=nonlocal damage, result=U, name=D
>>perElement, elSet=all, result=stress, quadraturePoint=0:8, name=stressForAMR

*step, solver=S
maxInc=0.5, minInc=1e-4, maxNumInc=50, maxIter=25,
>>dirichlet, name=x0, nSet=x0, field=displacement, 1=0.0, 2=0.0, 3=0.0
>>dirichlet, name=x1, nSet=x1, field=displacement, 3=0.0005

*Node
